---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { articleSchema } from '../../lib/techarticle.js';
import { reviewedByJabin } from '../../lib/person.js';

// Article for this career guide. Dates come from git via the same map the
// sitemap uses, so dateModified always equals this URL's sitemap lastmod;
// author and publisher are the Organization @id (see src/lib/techarticle.js).
// reviewedBy is a WebPage property in schema.org, so it sits on this page's
// WebPage node, which the Article names as its mainEntityOfPage.
const jsonLd = articleSchema({
  sourcePath: 'src/pages/careers/roughneck.astro',
  path: '/careers/roughneck',
  headline: "Roughneck Job: Rig Floor Duties, Pay and Promotion",
  description: "What a roughneck (floorhand) does on the rig floor: connections and trips, tours and hitches, pay, certifications, and the path up to derrickhand and driller.",
  lang: 'en',
  extra: { mainEntityOfPage: { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/careers/roughneck#webpage" } },
});

const webPageLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/careers/roughneck#webpage",
  "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/careers/roughneck",
  "name": "Roughneck Job: Duties, Pay and Promotion",
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "isPartOf": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/#website" },
  "mainEntity": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/careers/roughneck#article" },
  "reviewedBy": reviewedByJabin(),
};

---
<BaseLayout
  title="Roughneck Job: Duties, Pay and Promotion"
  description="What a roughneck (floorhand) does on the rig floor: connections and trips, tours and hitches, pay, certifications, and the path up to derrickhand and driller."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Careers', href: '/careers' }, { label: 'Roughneck', href: '/careers/roughneck' }]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  extraJsonLd={[webPageLd]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/careers/roughneck"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/careers/roughneck" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Careers — Entry Level</div>
    <h1>The Roughneck <span>Job</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">No experience required. This is the most accessible skilled-trade entry point in the oil and gas industry — here's exactly how to get in, what it pays, and what the job actually involves.</p>
    <div class="page-meta">
      <span class="page-meta-item">Entry Pay: <strong>$45K–$70K/yr</strong></span>
      <span class="page-meta-item">Time to Hire: <strong>2–8 weeks</strong></span>
      <span class="page-meta-item">Education: <strong>HS Diploma / GED</strong></span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="what-a-roughneck-actually-does">What a Roughneck Actually Does</h2>
        <p>A roughneck works directly on the rig floor, handling every part of the drilling process that involves the pipe itself. That means operating the tongs and iron roughneck to make up and break out pipe connections, running the catwalk that feeds pipe up to the floor, handling drill collars and BHA components, and keeping the rig floor clean and safe during operations. Almost nobody walks straight into this role — most hands spend 1–2 years as a <a href="/careers/roustabout"><strong>roustabout</strong></a> first, the true entry-level position, before earning a promotion to the floor. See the <a href="/careers/roustabout">full roustabout guide</a> for how that first stage works and how to get hired into it.</p>
        <p>It's physically demanding work — expect to lift, pull, and carry heavy equipment in 12-hour tours, often in extreme heat, cold, or offshore conditions. But it's also the foundation of every drilling career. Nearly every <a href="/careers/toolpusher">toolpusher</a>, driller, and rig manager started exactly here, and the <a href="/pillars/rig-floor-leadership">rig floor leadership guide</a> maps that supervisory career ladder from driller upward.</p>

        <h2 id="what-youll-actually-be-paid">What You'll Actually Be Paid</h2>
        <p>Entry-level roustabouts earn roughly <span class="highlight">$38,000–$55,000/year</span> onshore. Roughnecks on the drill floor earn <span class="highlight">$45,000–$70,000/year</span>, which works out to roughly <span class="highlight">$22–$34/hour</span> against a 12-hour tour — see the full <a href="/careers/salary#hourly-per-diem">hourly and per diem breakdown</a> for how that converts and what land contractors typically add on top in daily per diem (commonly $75–$150/day) for crews traveling to a remote location. Offshore roles pay 20–40% more than onshore equivalents due to isolation premiums and rotational schedules (commonly 14/14 or 28/28 — two or four weeks on, matched by time off), and generally carry no separate per diem since room and board on the installation is already free. Overtime is common and can meaningfully boost annual earnings beyond base pay.</p>
        <p>Drilling contractors (Nabors, Helmerich & Payne, Transocean, Noble, Patterson-UTI) post roughneck openings directly on their own careers pages, and energy staffing agencies (Airswift, Brunel, NES Fircroft) run their own listings too — those same agencies that placed you as a roustabout are usually the fastest route to a roughneck opening once you've put in the time on the floor.</p>

        <h2 id="the-physical-reality">The Physical Reality</h2>
        <p>This job is not for everyone. You'll work 12-hour shifts, often overnight, in extreme weather — desert heat in the Permian, freezing cold in North Dakota, humidity offshore in the Gulf. You'll lift heavy equipment repeatedly. Falls, pinch points, and dropped objects are real hazards, which is why safety culture on a rig is taken extremely seriously — not as a formality.</p>

        <h2 id="what-new-hands-get-wrong">What New Hands Get Wrong First</h2>
        <p>Jabin Travis, the 30-year driller who <a href="/about/jabin-travis">reviews our safety-critical content</a>, flags two mistakes he sees constantly on new crews:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Misreading rigging capacity.</strong> A lifting ring or master link stamped with a rating — say, 7 tons — is rated for <em>that number, total</em>, no matter how many sling legs are attached. New hands often assume a 4-leg sling multiplies the rating (7 × 4 = 28 tons); it doesn't. Always read the maximum stamped on the ring itself, not a number you calculate from the leg count.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Not knowing where to keep their hands.</strong> Around moving pipe, tongs, and rigging, hand placement is something experienced hands do almost without thinking — and something green hands genuinely have to be taught, not left to figure out. Knowing where your hands are, and keeping them clear of pinch points, is a habit that has to be built deliberately in the first weeks on the floor.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="what-comes-next">Next Rung: Derrickhand</h2>
        <p>After 1–3 years as a roughneck, the next step up is <strong>derrickhand</strong> (also called derrickman) — working from the <a href="/glossary#monkey-board">monkey board</a> high in the derrick, guiding pipe stands into the fingers during trips, and taking on responsibility for the mud system instead of just the floor. It's a real step up in both pay and trust: derrickhands typically earn <span class="highlight">$55,000–$90,000/year</span>, against a roughneck's $45,000–$70,000, and it's usually the point where a hand begins <strong>IWCF Level 2</strong> well control training — the first formal certification gate on the path toward driller.</p>
        <p>There's no separate hiring process for this one. It's a promotion earned on the same rig or with the same contractor, based on the same things that got you off roustabout duty in the first place: reliability, safety record, and demonstrated competence with the equipment. From derrickhand, the path continues to assistant driller and eventually driller. See the full <a href="/careers/driller">path to becoming a driller</a> for what comes after this stage.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="toc">
          <div class="toc-title">On This Page</div>
          <a href="#what-a-roughneck-actually-does">What a Roughneck Does</a>
          <a href="#what-youll-actually-be-paid">Expected Pay</a>
          <a href="#the-physical-reality">Physical Requirements</a>
          <a href="#what-new-hands-get-wrong">What New Hands Get Wrong</a>
          <a href="#what-comes-next">Next Rung: Derrickhand</a>
        </div>

        <div class="card" style="margin-top:20px;border:1px solid var(--iron);border-radius:4px;">
          <h3>Required Certifications</h3>
          <p style="margin-bottom:12px;">✅ High School Diploma / GED<br>✅ BOSIET (offshore only)<br>✅ Basic first aid / CPR (often provided)<br>⬜ IWCF Well Control (not required at entry)</p>
          <a href="/careers/certifications" class="card-link">Full Certification Guide →</a>
        </div>

        <div class="card" style="margin-top:1px;border:1px solid var(--iron);border-radius:0 0 4px 4px;">
          <h3>Related Reading</h3>
          <a href="/careers/roustabout" class="card-link">→ How to Become a Roustabout</a><br><br>
          <a href="/blog/roustabout-to-driller-timeline" class="card-link">→ Roustabout to Driller: Realistic Timeline</a><br><br>
          <a href="/careers/salary" class="card-link">→ Full Salary Guide</a><br><br>
          <a href="/careers/driller" class="card-link">→ How to Become a Driller</a><br><br>
          <a href="/blog/rig-floor-ppe-guide" class="card-link">→ Rig Floor PPE Guide</a><br><br>
          <a href="/safety" class="card-link">→ Rig Safety Overview</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
